# PENNSYLVANIA MEDICAL BILL DEFENSE TOOLKIT & LEGAL REFERENCE
**Action Guide and Statutory Framework for Pennsylvania Consumer Advocacy**

---

## ⚠️ CRITICAL WARNINGS & PROCEDURAL SAFEGUARDS

- **NEVER IGNORE COURT SUMMONS OR COMPLAINTS:** Expiration of Pennsylvania's $4$-year statute of limitations ($\text{42 Pa.C.S. § 5525(a)}$) does **not** automatically dismiss a lawsuit. Under **Pa.R.C.P. 1030**, the statute of limitations is an **Affirmative Defense** that must be explicitly raised under "New Matter" in a formal Answer. Failure to plead it constitutes a permanent waiver of the defense.
- **DO NOT MAKE PARTIAL PAYMENTS ON TIME-BARRED DEBTS:** Under Pennsylvania common law (the **Acknowledgment Doctrine**), voluntary partial payments act as constructive acknowledgment of a debt, inferring a promise to pay the balance and resetting the $4$-year limitation clock to day zero (*Huntingdon Fin. Corp. v. Int'l Packaging Corp.*, 659 A.2d 1054 (Pa. Super. 1995); *Cole v. Lawrence*, 701 A.2d 987 (Pa. Super. 1997)). Maintain complete payment stoppage ($\$0.00$) during active disputes.
- **DISTINGUISH LIVE DEBTS FROM TIME-BARRED DEBTS:** Blanket non-payment on live, enforceable contracts within the $4$-year window invites default litigation and credit damage. Strategic non-payment applies specifically to disputed, non-compliant, or time-barred claims.
- **PRESERVE INSURANCE APPEAL TIMELINES:** Statutory external reviews must be requested following internal plan exhaustion. Internal ERISA/ACA appeals must be filed within $180$ days of an adverse benefit determination (EOB).

